-- Add datadog_rum_identified_user_orgs, the user-to-organisation link table, and
-- repoint datadog_rum_identified_users_view's organisation columns at it.
--
-- WHY: @usr.organization is a per-SESSION attribute and a Conductor user can
-- belong to several organisations, so the relationship is many-to-many. The
-- registry stored one row per user with a single user_organization column, and
-- DeriveEntries collapsed each user onto their newest organisation. Measured on
-- 2026-09-02: count(distinct user_organization) returned 17 where DataDog had 24
-- over the same three collected weeks. One production account has been seen
-- under 8 organisations and four more under 2.
--
-- Design: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-02-datadog-user-org-many-to-many-design.md
-- Plan:   docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-02-datadog-user-org-many-to-many.md
--
-- Neon's HTTP endpoint takes ONE STATEMENT PER REQUEST, so there is no
-- multi-statement transaction here. Send statement 1, then statement 2. The
-- CREATE TABLE is additive and cannot affect existing data; if the view
-- replacement fails, the old view stays and the new table is simply unread.
--
-- Both statements were dry-run against the live schema on 2026-09-03 inside an
-- aborting DO block and returned P0001 "DRY RUN OK" with nothing persisted and
-- the view still reporting 12 columns (no 42P16).
--
-- DDL APPLIED 2026-09-03 by the maintainer. Verified: table created with all 7
-- columns (organization NOT NULL, first_seen_week nullable), constraint
-- datadog_rum_identified_user_orgs_unique = UNIQUE (service, env, user_id,
-- organization) present, and pg_get_viewdef confirms the view now reads the link
-- table. No regression: datadog_rum_identified still 6 rows, view still 12
-- columns, registry still 57 rows, session_count and all user columns unchanged
-- (24/39/54 on conductor.r1, 3 on dapr-ops-dashboard).
--
-- *** DATA STEP STILL OUTSTANDING as of 2026-09-03. *** The link table is empty
-- (0 rows, no collection_date), so new_orgs/total_orgs currently read 0 on every
-- week, DOWN from 17. This is the documented window between statement 2 and the
-- collector's real run, and it is open now. Nothing is corrupted and no count
-- other than the organisation columns moved. Close it by running step 0 then
-- step 3 above. Delete this paragraph once total_orgs reads 23 / 3.
--
-- ORDER OF OPERATIONS -- do these in this order:
--   0. Run the collector with skipStorage:true FIRST. It needs no table, and it
--      is the only chance to exercise StoreOrgAsync's statement before it
--      writes. Expect, for the 2026-08-10..2026-08-31 lookback:
--        conductor-ui@conductor.r1.diagrid.io:       60 pairs over 23 distinct organisations
--        conductor-ui@dapr-ops-dashboard.diagrid.io:  4 pairs over 3 distinct organisations
--      and NO 'WARNING: "orgs" request ... at the group-by limit' line.
--      If the lookback has moved past 2026-08-31, re-derive these from DataDog
--      by grouping [@usr.id, @usr.organization] per env over the three complete
--      weeks the run actually covered.
--   1. Statement 1 below (CREATE TABLE).
--   2. Statement 2 below (CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW) -- copy it verbatim from
--      postgres/postgres_schema.psql, which is the source of truth. It is
--      reproduced here for convenience only.
--   3. Run the collector with skipStorage:false immediately. Between step 2 and
--      this, new_orgs/total_orgs read 0, because the table starts empty.
--   4. Verification queries at the foot of this file.
--
-- NO BACKFILL from user_organization. Its 53 non-null values carry no
-- trustworthy first-seen week, and `on conflict do nothing` would then stop a
-- real run from ever correcting a guessed one.
--
-- PRE-MIGRATION BASELINE, captured 2026-09-03:
--   datadog_rum_identified_user_orgs               does not exist
--   datadog_rum_identified                         6 rows        <- must still be 6
--   datadog_rum_identified_users (conductor-ui)    57 rows, 53 with an org,
--                                                  17 distinct orgs, 30 with install_date
--   datadog_rum_identified_users_view              12 columns    <- must still be 12
--   view total_orgs                                17 on conductor.r1, 2 on dapr-ops-dashboard
--   view total_users                               54 on conductor.r1, 3 on dapr-ops-dashboard  <- must NOT change

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- STATEMENT 1
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- One row per (user, organisation) pair ever observed. @usr.organization is a
-- per-SESSION attribute and a Conductor user can belong to several
-- organisations, so this relationship is many-to-many: as of 2026-09-02 one
-- account has been seen under 8 organisations and four more under 2. Count
-- organisations HERE, never with count(distinct user_organization) on
-- datadog_rum_identified_users -- that column keeps only one of them and
-- undercounted 17 against a true 24 when this table was added.
--
-- Holds a user id joined to an organisation identifier: PII, like
-- datadog_rum_identified_users. Never print or log these values -- this
-- repository is public and its GitHub Actions run logs are world-readable.
-- Report from datadog_rum_identified_users_view, which exposes counts.
--
-- first_seen_week is the Monday of the ISO week the PAIR was first observed
-- in, or NULL when that was the oldest week the run collected -- meaning the
-- pair existed at or before the edge of what was queried and its true first
-- week is unknowable. Same convention as install_date.
--
-- env is part of the key, so a pair active on both deployment hosts is stored
-- twice: count(*) is a row count, NOT a pair count. count(distinct
-- organization) is safe across envs; counting pairs needs
-- count(distinct (user_id, organization)).
CREATE TABLE datadog_rum_identified_user_orgs (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    service VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    env VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    user_id VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL,
    organization VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL,
    first_seen_week DATE,
    collection_date TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
    CONSTRAINT datadog_rum_identified_user_orgs_unique
        UNIQUE (service, env, user_id, organization)
);

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- STATEMENT 2 -- verbatim from postgres/postgres_schema.psql
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW datadog_rum_identified_users_view AS
WITH first_week AS (
    SELECT service, env, MIN(week_start) AS week_start
    FROM datadog_rum_identified
    GROUP BY service, env
),
seeded AS (
    SELECT u.service,
           u.env,
           COALESCE(u.install_date, f.week_start) AS week_start,
           u.user_email,
           u.user_organization
    FROM datadog_rum_identified_users u
    JOIN first_week f
      ON f.service = u.service AND f.env = u.env
),
-- An organisation is new in the week its FIRST user appeared, not in every
-- week another of its users shows up. Counting distinct orgs per week would
-- count the same org again each time it gained a user.
--
-- Derived from datadog_rum_identified_user_orgs, not from
-- datadog_rum_identified_users.user_organization: that column keeps only one
-- of a user's organisations, which undercounted total_orgs 17 against a true
-- 24. The COALESCE seeds an unknown first week from the earliest week on
-- record, exactly as `seeded` does for install_date, so both halves of this
-- view treat unknown history the same way.
--
-- CAVEAT -- new_orgs/total_orgs are NOT stable across runs. The COALESCE runs
-- BEFORE the MIN, and f.week_start is the earliest week ever collected for that
-- (service, env), which never advances; first_seen_week is NULL for any pair
-- first seen in the CURRENT run's boundary week, which advances every Monday.
-- So an organisation first observed months from now, in that run's boundary
-- week, is reported as new in the earliest collected week instead -- and an
-- organisation with one known week plus a later NULL pair is dragged back to
-- that floor. `seeded` has the identical flaw for new_users today, so this
-- mirrors existing behaviour rather than adding a new defect. The fix is
-- COALESCE(MIN(o.first_seen_week), MIN(f.week_start)), which keeps an
-- organisation on its earliest KNOWN week; it is deliberately out of scope
-- here and belongs with the install_date rework.
org_first_week AS (
    SELECT o.service,
           o.env,
           o.organization,
           MIN(COALESCE(o.first_seen_week, f.week_start)) AS week_start
    FROM datadog_rum_identified_user_orgs o
    JOIN first_week f
      ON f.service = o.service AND f.env = o.env
    GROUP BY o.service, o.env, o.organization
),
-- The row spine. Every COLLECTED week appears, not just the weeks in which
-- some user was first seen -- otherwise a week with real sessions but no new
-- users produces no row at all, and its session_count has nowhere to land.
-- dapr-ops-dashboard had exactly that: three collected weeks, one view row.
-- Keying on collected weeks alone would be simpler, but a user whose
-- install_date week somehow had no aggregate row (an aggregate write failing
-- while the registry write succeeded) would then vanish from the view and
-- undercount total_users. The union cannot lose a user; session_count simply
-- comes back NULL for such a week.
week_spine AS (
    SELECT service, env, week_start FROM datadog_rum_identified
    UNION
    SELECT service, env, week_start FROM seeded
    UNION
    -- Third arm for the same reason as the second: the spine must not be able
    -- to lose a row. A user can gain an organisation in a week they were not
    -- themselves first seen in, and if that week had no aggregate row the LEFT
    -- JOIN below would drop the organisation and undercount total_orgs.
    SELECT service, env, week_start FROM org_first_week
),
user_weeks AS (
    SELECT service,
           env,
           week_start,
           COUNT(*) AS new_users,
           COUNT(*) FILTER (
               WHERE user_email IS NULL OR user_email NOT ILIKE '%@diagrid.io'
           ) AS new_external_users
    FROM seeded
    GROUP BY service, env, week_start
),
org_weeks AS (
    SELECT service, env, week_start, COUNT(*) AS new_orgs
    FROM org_first_week
    GROUP BY service, env, week_start
)
-- Column ORDER matters here: CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW can only append columns,
-- never insert or rename one, so anything added must go at the end however
-- much better it would read elsewhere. Reordering this list makes the next
-- deployment fail with 42P16.
--
-- Mind the provenance mix in these columns. new_*/total_* are derived from the
-- registry and are exact counts of registered users and organisations, with
-- the external variants excluding @diagrid.io. session_count,
-- unique_user_count and org_count are copied from datadog_rum_identified and
-- come from DataDog `cardinality` aggregations over ALL authenticated
-- accounts, internal included, with no external variant available. Charting
-- unique_user_count against total_external_users compares two different
-- populations measured two different ways.
SELECT s.service,
       s.env,
       s.week_start,
       COALESCE(u.new_users, 0) AS new_users,
       COALESCE(u.new_external_users, 0) AS new_external_users,
       SUM(COALESCE(u.new_users, 0)) OVER w AS total_users,
       SUM(COALESCE(u.new_external_users, 0)) OVER w AS total_external_users,
       COALESCE(o.new_orgs, 0) AS new_orgs,
       SUM(COALESCE(o.new_orgs, 0)) OVER w AS total_orgs,
       a.session_count,
       a.unique_user_count,
       a.org_count
FROM week_spine s
LEFT JOIN user_weeks u
  ON u.service = s.service AND u.env = s.env AND u.week_start = s.week_start
LEFT JOIN org_weeks o
  ON o.service = s.service AND o.env = s.env AND o.week_start = s.week_start
LEFT JOIN datadog_rum_identified a
  ON a.service = s.service AND a.env = s.env AND a.week_start = s.week_start
WINDOW w AS (PARTITION BY s.service, s.env ORDER BY s.week_start);

